      Fig. 3 | Perturbation phenotypic profiling of scMIR embeddings. a . Schematic overview of phenotypic inference from perturbation image embeddings. Chemical perturbations
    </caption>
    <description>
      <content>SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

The image is a multi-panel scientific figure about image-based profiling of chemical perturbations.

Panel a shows a workflow: a multi-well plate is imaged by microscopy, producing cellular fluorescence images. These images are passed through a “Feature extractor” model/interface to generate “Image embeddings.” Embeddings from wells of chemical perturbation are then compared, with dashed circles indicating wells associated with the same chemical perturbation.

Panel b is titled “Performance comparison on BBBC021” and contains three scatter plots comparing methods by Mean Average Precision (mAP) on the x-axis and Folds of Enrichment on the y-axis. The subplots are:
- “Treatment level-MoAs”
- “Well level-Compounds”
- “Well level-MoAs”

Methods in the legend are CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR. scMIR appears as the red point and has the highest performance in the shown plots.

Panel c is titled “Treatment representations clustering” for BBBC021. It shows a UMAP scatter plot with axes UMAP1 and UMAP2. Points represent control wells, well-level profiles, and treatment-level profiles. Labeled biological/chemical mechanism clusters include Epithelial, Protein synthesis, Actin disruptors, DNA damage, Kinase inhibitors, and Microtubule destabilizers.

Panel d is titled “Heatmap visualization of of AZ-A perturbation representations” for BBBC021. It shows a heatmap with DMSO and multiple dose/replicate-like row labels on the left, and a color scale from about -3 to +3 on the right. The heatmap visualizes learned perturbation representation values or embedding features across conditions.</content>
    </description>
  </picture>
  <picture>
    <location value="91"/>
    <location value="320"/>
    <location value="422"/>
    <location value="438"/>
    <description>
      <content>SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

The image is a single heatmap panel showing many rows of features/samples against labeled columns of biological taxa. The columns include an initial `DNSO` label followed by microbial genus/species-style labels such as `A2 (4)`, `anisomycin`, `desmococcus`, `emetic`, `equitabilis`, `episodica`, `flavumaurantiacum`, `minorygin`, `nocobacta`, `taylor`, and `viridimicis` variants with replicate/group numbers in parentheses.

Visible elements:
- One rectangular clustered-style heatmap, though no dendrograms are visible.
- X-axis labels are rotated vertically and densely packed.
- No visible y-axis labels.
- A vertical color bar at right ranges approximately from `-4` in blue through `0` white to `4` red.
- Color encoding appears to represent normalized abundance/expression/intensity values, with red indicating higher values and blue indicating lower values.
- The heatmap contains block-like patterns of red and blue across groups, suggesting differential signal across taxa/conditions.

Biological source objects:
- Likely microbial taxa or biological sample groups, based on genus/species-like labels.
- No cells, tissues, organisms, microscopy images, or molecular structures are directly visible.

Transformations/model interfaces:
- Data appear transformed into a scaled matrix or z-score-like heatmap due to the centered diverging color scale.
- No model architecture, workflow diagram, or interface is shown.

Findings visible from the figure:
- Several column groups show localized enrichment or depletion patterns.
- Strong red regions are visible near labels including `equitabilis`, `taylor`, and some `nocobacta`/related columns.
- Strong blue regions appear in multiple blocks, including early `A2/AZ` columns and some mid-to-late taxa groups.
- Exact quantitative findings cannot be determined beyond the color-scale patterns because row labels and caption are absent.</content>
    </description>
  </picture>
  <page_break/>
  <text>
    <location value="77"/>
