      Fig.  4 | a .  Schematic  illustration  of  hierarchical  feature  aggregation  on  the  Human Protein Atlas  (HPA)  dataset.  Single-cell  image  embeddings  are  first  extracted  from individual microscopy images and then aggregated to obtain higher-level representations at multiple biological scales, including cell line-level, protein localization-level, and protein-level features. b . Similarity matrix of protein localization groups computed from scMIR-derived protein-level features, with expertdefined  hierarchical  organelle  annotations  indicated  (red  and  yellow). c .  Cell-line similarity  matrices  derived  from  bulk  mRNA  expression  (left)  and  morphology-
    </caption>
    <description>
      <content>SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Multi-panel biological/computational figure about scMIR embeddings from HPA single-cell images.

Panel a: “Feature aggregation on HPA images”
- Shows multiple single-cell fluorescence microscopy images as biological source objects.
- Pipeline diagram: single-cell images pass through a “Feature extractor,” producing feature vectors.
- Features are combined by “Feature aggregation.”
- Outputs are three embedding types: “Cell line embeddings,” “Protein localization embeddings,” and “Protein embeddings.”
- Model/interface elements are schematic blocks and arrows, not code or UI.

Panel b: “scMIR protein localization embedding similarity heatmap”
- Heatmap of protein localization embedding similarity.
- Rows/columns are subcellular localization labels including nucleoli rim, nucleoli, nucleoli fibrillar center, vesicles, peroxisomes, lysosomes, endosomes, lipid droplets, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, cytoplasmic bodies, cytosol, centriolar satellite, centrosome, microtubule ends, microtubules, intermediate filaments, actin filaments, focal adhesion sites, cell junctions, plasma membrane, mitochondria, nuclear membrane, nucleoplasm, nuclear bodies, and nuclear speckles.
- Red outlined regions annotate localization groups: Nucleoli, Endomembrane system, Cytosol, Cytoskeleton, Plasma membrane, Mitochondria, and Nucleus.
- Darker blue blocks indicate higher similarity within some localization groups.

Panel c: “Cell line similarity heatmaps based on mRNA and scMIR embeddings”
- Two side-by-side heatmaps compare cell line similarity.
- Left heatmap labeled “mRNA.”
- Right heatmap labeled “scMIR embeddings.”
- Cell lines listed include NB4, SK-MEL-30, MCF-7, Hep-G2, A-549, OE19, HaCaT, SuSa, PC-3, HeLa, CACO-2, EFO-21, HDLM-2, U-251MG, A-431, SiHa, GAMG, U2OS, BJ, hTCEpi, RPTEC/TERT1, HUVEC/TERT2, HCN-M2, ASC52telo, hTERT-RPE1, AF22, RT-4, RH30, HEK293, REH, HAP1, SH-SY5Y, THP-1, JURKAT, HEL, and K-562.
- Color bar shows cosine similarity from -1.00 to 1.00.

Panel d: “Mantel statistic comparison”
- Bar chart comparing Mantel statistic for “Cell line” and “Protein localization.”
- Methods shown by color: CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR.
- scMIR has the highest visible values: 0.29 for Cell line and 0.31 for Protein localization.
- Other labeled values: Cell line 0.22, 0.20, 0.24; Protein localization 0.14, 0.16, 0.17.

Panel e: “Comparison of protein interaction prediction performance”
- Bar chart comparing methods: CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR.
- Metrics: Spearman, Kendall, and TopBottomSep@10%.
- scMIR has the highest visible performance on all metrics: Spearman 4.69, Kendall 3.14, TopBottomSep@10% 2.70.
- Other visible values: Spearman 0.96, 1.14, 2.58; Kendall 0.64, 0.77, 1.72; TopBottomSep@10% 0.36, 0.44, 0.66.

Overall finding shown: scMIR embeddings derived from HPA single-cell images preserve biologically meaningful subcellular localization and cell-line similarity structure and outperform the comparison image embedding methods in the plotted protein interaction prediction metrics.</content>
